From: Domenico D'Arrigo <d.darrigo@outlook.it>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 23:13:17 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] sb/intel/lynxpoint: Leave PM1 and GPE0 events to the OS in
 SCI mode

With SCI_EN set, PM1 and GPE0 events raise an SCI for the OS, not an
SMI#, but their bits in SMI_STS still show them until the OS clears
them. The SMI handler calls the PM1 and GPE0 sub handlers for any SMI#
with those bits set: an APMC command while the OS has a power button
event pending powers the system off, and the GPE0 status of the OS is
cleared.

On S3 resume the firmware leaves PM1_STS and GPE0_STS to the OS since
the patch "Leave the wake status to the OS on S3 resume", and ramstage
sends APMC commands before the OS resumes: finalize from lpc_final()
and, with vboot A/B, the lock of the other slot from SMMSTORE. The
ThinkPad EC wakes the system through the power button line, so
PWRBTN_STS is set at every wake, by the power button, the lid or a key:
the first APMC command powered the laptop off, under Linux and macOS.

Ignore PM1 and GPE0 in the SMI handler when SCI_EN is set, as
soc/intel/common does since commit 517750745f7f ("soc/intel/common/
block/smm: Ignore PM1 and GPE0 events in SCI mode"). Should those
events alone have raised the SMI#, which SCI_EN rules out, clear them
as before, without powering off, so that the SMI# does not repeat.

TEST=Build the ThinkPad W541 (mrc and nri); on the laptop, S3 entered
under Linux and macOS and left with the power button, the lid and a
key: the system resumes instead of powering off.

Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_018n3yuWwDLW63F4vdf7ZFTv
---
 src/southbridge/intel/lynxpoint/pch.h        |  2 ++
 src/southbridge/intel/lynxpoint/smihandler.c | 21 ++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 23 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/southbridge/intel/lynxpoint/pch.h b/src/southbridge/intel/lynxpoint/pch.h
index 57e1cef2fc..2a622374be 100644
--- a/src/southbridge/intel/lynxpoint/pch.h
+++ b/src/southbridge/intel/lynxpoint/pch.h
@@ -593,6 +593,8 @@ void mainboard_config_rcba(void);
 #define   EOS		 (1 <<  1) // End of SMI (deassert SMI#)
 #define   GBL_SMI_EN	 (1 <<  0) // SMI# generation at all?
 #define SMI_STS		0x34
+#define   GPE0_STS_BIT	9 // GPE0 event: an SCI with SCI_EN set
+#define   PM1_STS_BIT	8 // PM1 event: an SCI with SCI_EN set
 #define ALT_GP_SMI_EN	0x38
 #define ALT_GP_SMI_STS	0x3a
 #define GPE_CNTL	0x42
diff --git a/src/southbridge/intel/lynxpoint/smihandler.c b/src/southbridge/intel/lynxpoint/smihandler.c
index 68b212997e..d77268801a 100644
--- a/src/southbridge/intel/lynxpoint/smihandler.c
+++ b/src/southbridge/intel/lynxpoint/smihandler.c
@@ -578,6 +578,27 @@ void southbridge_smi_handler(void)
 	 */
 	smi_sts = clear_smi_status();
 
+	/*
+	 * With SCI_EN set, PM1 and GPE0 events raise an SCI for the OS, not an
+	 * SMI#, but SMI_STS still shows them until the OS clears them. Leave
+	 * them to the OS: any SMI# meanwhile, such as an APMC command, would
+	 * clear its GPE0 status and turn its pending power button event into a
+	 * power off. On S3 resume PM1_STS holds PWRBTN_STS when the power
+	 * button, or the EC through it, woke the system, and ramstage sends
+	 * APMC commands before the OS resumes (finalize, the vboot slot lock).
+	 * Should those events alone have raised this SMI#, clear them, or it
+	 * would come again.
+	 */
+	if (inl(get_pmbase() + PM1_CNT) & SCI_EN) {
+		const u32 os_events = smi_sts & (1 << PM1_STS_BIT | 1 << GPE0_STS_BIT);
+
+		smi_sts &= ~os_events;
+		if (os_events && !smi_sts) {
+			clear_pm1_status();
+			clear_gpe_status();
+		}
+	}
+
 	/* Call SMI sub handler for each of the status bits */
 	for (i = 0; i < 31; i++) {
 		if (smi_sts & (1 << i)) {
